## H3 — Stack buffer claim vs slice

| Artifact | What it says / does |
|----------|---------------------|
| `SPRINT.oot` 7.2 | “`c_sys_exec_last_arg` **same**” as 7.1 “**stack buffer** (no per-char OoStr leak)” |
| Source comment L121 | “one `str_slice` for the last arg span” |
| `.oo` implementation | scalars (`i`, `j`, `n`, `last_start`, `in_str`, `depth`) + **one** `str_slice` — **no** `char buf[N]`, no `List` inside the fn |
| Runtime | `OoStr out = oo_str_slice(args, last_start, n)` — **heap copy of the span** |
| `t1_memory.oot` M-SG3 | already **FIXED** — `str_slice(args, last_start, n)` |
| `s71_memory.oot` H3 | sibling: do **not** FAIL for missing C `char[]` |

Honesty: the **CRIT** (no per-char leak) is met. The **mechanism name** “stack buffer” was never landed and cannot be landed in `.oo` without a byte cursor / C helper. Path-A floor = **slice**, not a C VLA.

**H3: wording residual only. Do not FAIL the memory floor for missing stack `char[]`.**

